<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="A detector that misses an instruction and an executor that allows an unauthorised action are separate failures, so a test should record both gates instead of treating a detection score as the whole defence.">

<p>The user asked an agent for a summary. The retrieved page it reads tries to hand it a new instruction, an unrelated action. A defensive test of that situation has three things to look at: what reaches the model, what the model proposes, and what the application permits. A detector's verdict covers only the first.</p>
<p>Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>prompt-injection guidance</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes indirect instructions arriving through third-party content. It recommends keeping untrusted material separate and limiting access to actions and sensitive data. We use a constructed example to show how to report those boundaries, and it is not a security result.</p>
<h2>A fixture needs a prohibited outcome</h2>
<p>Each fixture starts from the legitimate task and the one action it must not cause. The documents are synthetic and the executor is simulated, so a test can propose a harmless dummy operation and record whether it would be authorised, without sending messages, changing accounts or touching real secrets.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Gate in a constructed test","Count"]} rows={examples['prompt-injection-permission-tests']} caption="Invented defensive test ledger, not observed attack results. All operations are assumed to use harmless fixtures and a simulated executor." />
<p>Here the screen detects eighteen of twenty malicious fixtures. Two reach the model and produce prohibited proposals, and the executor rejects both. Detection is 90% in the invented set. Zero prohibited actions execute because the example assumes an independent denial gate, and that zero is an assumption, not an observed defence rate.</p>
<h2>Proposing is not the same as being permitted</h2>
<p>The permitted action comes from the user's task and the application's policy, and the executor checks the operation, target and scope before running anything. A well-formed tool call from the model does not show that the user authorised it.</p>
<p>For consequential work, a proposed operation should be concrete enough to review: which target, which change and which evidence supports it. Approval stays tied to that operation, so a vague permission to continue cannot quietly stretch to a different destination or effect.</p>
<p>Denied proposals are evidence even when nothing runs, because they show how the model read the untrusted text. A screen that passes a fixture, on the other hand, does not prove the model followed it. The sequence is worth keeping whole instead of squeezed into one success label.</p>
<h2>Benign work has to pass too</h2>
<p>Ordinary documents contain quotations, instructions meant for humans and examples of commands. A defence that blocks the legitimate task on those inputs has a usability cost, so benign-task acceptance gets its own stated rule, separate from the malicious-fixture outcomes.</p>
<p>The cases should come from the actual workflow: retrieved pages for a research assistant, document text for an extractor, tool output for a coding helper. Fixtures are fit to publish only if they are synthetic or cleared for it. Your own results log needs outcomes and configuration, not private source content.</p>
<p>A change of model, prompt, tool wrapper or permission set means repeating the test, since the same fixture may now travel through a different boundary. The executor policy version belongs with the result, so the outcome is not credited to the model name alone.</p>
<h2>What the test leaves uncovered</h2>
<p>Denied writes do not show that no sensitive information was exposed in a response. A read operation can still fetch material the task should not reach. The data-access boundary needs its own review, and the agent should not be given material it does not need.</p>
<p>A small fixture set cannot establish general resistance to prompt injection either. The report should give the cases, assumptions and observed actions, and a clean run is not a claim of immunity.</p>
<p>A release record is most useful when it says what the agent saw, what it proposed, which gate allowed or denied it and whether the legitimate task finished. That supports a narrower, more actionable decision than a detector score alone.</p>
<PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
